paste:

  whisper: ---- moving the player: speed changes the place, gravity changes the speed

  
type fast:
  to run and jump:
    if key held "left":
      set speed x to speed x minus 0.8
      set facing to -1
    otherwise if key held "right":
      set speed x to speed x plus 0.8
      set facing to 1
    otherwise:
      set speed x to speed x times 0.8
    set speed x to clamp speed x between -6 and 6
    if key pressed "space" and on ground:
      set speed y to -13
    set speed y to smaller of (speed y plus 0.6) and 14
  and the universe said i love you
keys: enter
say:
  This is the most important part of the game. Let's go through it slowly.
line: 141
zoom: lines 128-141
spot: lines 129-131
say:
  If the left key is held, speed x goes down by point eight each frame, so the player speeds up toward the left. Not all at once, but a little more every frame. That's what makes running feel smooth.
spot: lines 132-134
say:
  The right key does the same toward the right.
spot: lines 135-136
say:
  With neither key held, speed x is multiplied by point eight every frame. So the player slows down, and glides to a stop, like friction.
spot: line 137
say:
  Clamp keeps speed x between minus six and six, so the player can't run faster than six dots a frame.
spot: lines 138-139
say:
  Jumping. Key pressed is yes for only one frame per press. And only on the ground: no jumping in mid air. A jump sets speed y to minus thirteen. On a screen, up is less, so minus thirteen means thirteen dots up, every frame.
spot: line 140
say:
  And this one line is gravity. Every frame, speed y grows by point six. So a jump starts fast upward, slows, stops at the top, and comes back down faster and faster, in a smooth curve. Smaller of keeps the fall from getting faster than fourteen.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  to overlaps, given p (a platform), returning yes or no:
    return player x plus 30 exceeds the x of p and player x falls below the x of p plus the width of p and player y plus 40 exceeds the y of p and player y falls below the y of p plus the height of p
  and the universe said i love you

  to test overlapping:
    set player x to 100
    set player y to 430
    check overlaps with platform at with (0, 460, 760, 80)
    set player y to 380
    check not overlaps with platform at with (0, 460, 760, 80)
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 153
zoom: lines 143-153
spot: lines 143-144
say:
  Overlaps asks whether the player's box and a platform's box overlap. The player is thirty dots wide and forty high. Two boxes overlap when each one's left edge is left of the other's right edge, and each one's top is above the other's bottom. All four must be true.
spot: lines 147-153
say:
  And a test for it: standing in the ground overlaps it, and fifty dots higher doesn't. The test button runs it.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  to move across:
    set player x to player x plus speed x
    for each p in platforms:
      if overlaps with p:
        if speed x exceeds 0: set player x to the x of p minus 30
        otherwise: set player x to the x of p plus the width of p
        set speed x to 0
    set player x to clamp player x between 0 and 3170
  and the universe said i love you

  to move up and down:
    set on ground to no
    set player y to player y plus speed y
    for each p in platforms:
      if overlaps with p:
        if speed y exceeds 0:
          set player y to the y of p minus 40
          set on ground to yes
        otherwise:
          set player y to the y of p plus the height of p
        set speed y to 0
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 163
zoom: lines 155-163
spot: line 156
say:
  Now the moving itself. The trick is to move across and down separately, one after the other. First across: add speed x to player x.
spot: lines 157-161
say:
  Then, if that put the player inside a platform, push it back out. Moving right, the player's right side goes against the platform's left edge. Moving left, its left side goes against the platform's right edge. And speed x becomes zero: we hit a wall.
spot: line 162
say:
  And clamp keeps the player inside the level, from its left end to its right end.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
line: 175
zoom: lines 165-175
spot: lines 166-167
say:
  Then up and down, the same way. On ground starts as no, and speed y moves the player.
spot: lines 170-172
say:
  If the player was falling into a platform, its feet are put on the platform's top, and now, on ground is yes. That's the only place on ground ever becomes yes, so jumping only works after landing.
spot: lines 173-174
say:
  And if it was going up, it bumped its head: it's put just under the platform.
spot: line 175
say:
  Either way, speed y becomes zero.
say:
  Why separately? If we moved both at once, and ended up inside a corner of a platform, we couldn't tell whether we hit a wall or landed on a floor. One direction at a time, the answer is always clear.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  whisper: ---- coins: which ones are taken is kept in the bits of one number

  to is taken, given n (a number), returning yes or no:
    return ((coins taken shifted right by (n minus 1)) bitwise and 1) is 1
  and the universe said i love you

  to pick up coins:
    set middle to (player x plus 15) horizontal (player y plus 20) vertical
    for each n from 1 to the size of coins:
      if not is taken with n and the existence of item n of coins is within 30 of middle:
        set coins taken to coins taken bitwise or (1 shifted left by (n minus 1))
        set score to score plus 1
  and the universe said i love you
  
line: 190
zoom: lines 180-190
spot: line 181
say:
  Is taken looks at one bit of coins taken. Shifted right moves bit n to the end, and bitwise and one keeps only that bit.
spot: line 185
say:
  Picking up coins. Middle is the middle of the player: fifteen across and twenty down from its corner.
spot: lines 187-189
say:
  Any coin not taken yet, within thirty dots of the middle, is taken: bitwise or sets its bit, and the score goes up.
spot: off
keys: ctrl+end
paste:

  whisper: ---- dangers: spikes and bees are different kinds of things, but both can hurt and appear

  to hurt, for a spike, returning yes or no:
    return player x plus 30 exceeds x and player x falls below x plus width and player y plus 40 exceeds 440
  and the universe said i love you

  to appear, for a spike:
    set left to x minus camera
    for each k from 0 to width divided by 12 minus 1:
      etch a box with left plus k times 12 plus 3, 446, 6, 14, "#90a4ae"
      etch a box with left plus k times 12 plus 5, 440, 2, 6, "#cfd8dc"
  and the universe said i love you

  to hurt, for a bee, returning yes or no:
    return existence is within 26 of (player x plus 15) horizontal (player y plus 20) vertical
  and the universe said i love you

  to appear, for a bee:
    set spot to existence minus camera horizontal 0 vertical
    etch a circle with spot of horizontal, spot of vertical, 12, "#fdd835"
    etch a box with spot of horizontal minus 4, spot of vertical minus 12, 3, 24, "#212121"
    etch a box with spot of horizontal plus 3, spot of vertical minus 12, 3, 24, "#212121"
    set flap to 4 plus 3 times sine of (ticks times 2000)
    etch a circle with spot of horizontal, spot of vertical minus 12 minus flap, 7, "#e3f2fd"
  and the universe said i love you

  to appear, for a queen bee:
    set spot to existence minus camera horizontal 0 vertical
    etch a circle with spot of horizontal, spot of vertical, 16, "#fbc02d"
    etch a box with spot of horizontal minus 5, spot of vertical minus 16, 4, 32, "#212121"
    etch a box with spot of horizontal plus 3, spot of vertical minus 16, 4, 32, "#212121"
    etch a box with spot of horizontal minus 9, spot of vertical minus 30, 18, 8, crown
  and the universe said i love you

  to fly, for a bee, given t (a number):
    set existence to birth plus (sine of (t times 90) times reach) horizontal (sine of (t times 240) times 10) vertical
  and the universe said i love you

  to the dangers, returning a list of dangers:
    set all to a list of dangers
    for each s in spikes: add s to all
    for each b in bees: add b to all
    add queen to all
    return all
  and the universe said i love you

  to the danger touching, returning a danger:
    for each d in the dangers:
      if hurt d: return d
    return no danger
  and the universe said i love you
  
